---
import Pillar from '../../layouts/Pillar.astro';
import ChildGrid from '../../components/ChildGrid.astro';
import { CHILDREN } from '../../data/clusters';
import { SITE } from '../../consts';

const items = CHILDREN['gear'];
const schema = [{
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'Golf Equipment Guides: What to Buy, and When Not To',
  description: 'Buying guides and reviews for drivers, irons, wedges, putters, balls and rangefinders — with an honest account of what equipment can and cannot fix.',
  hasPart: items.map((i) => ({
    '@type': 'WebPage', name: i.title, url: new URL(i.href, SITE.url).href,
  })),
}];
---
<Pillar
  cluster="gear"
  title='Golf Equipment Guides: What to Buy, and When Not To'
  description='Buying guides and reviews for drivers, irons, wedges, putters, balls and rangefinders — with an honest account of what equipment can and cannot fix.'
  intro='Equipment can genuinely help. It cannot fix a swing fault, and the industry has a strong commercial interest in blurring that line. These guides try not to.'
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="pil-prose">
      <h2>Read this before you spend anything</h2>
      <p>
        Three things are true at the same time, and most gear content only admits the first:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Modern equipment is genuinely better</strong> than equipment from fifteen years ago, particularly in forgiveness on off-centre strikes.</li>
        <li><strong>Fitting matters more than model.</strong> A well-fitted mid-range club will outperform a poorly-fitted premium one, consistently.</li>
        <li><strong>Equipment cannot fix a swing fault.</strong> A draw-biased driver reduces a slice. It does not cure the out-to-in path producing it, and the fault will reappear the moment you change clubs.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout callout-key">
        <p class="callout-title">Our bias, stated openly</p>
        <p>
          We earn commission when you buy through our links, which gives us an obvious incentive
          to tell you to buy things. We manage it by publishing
          <a href="/gear/how-we-test/">how we test</a>, labelling the evidence behind every
          recommendation, and saying plainly when the answer is to keep what you own. Judge us
          against that.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Where the money is best spent</h2>
      <p>
        If you have a fixed budget and want the most strokes per pound, this is roughly the order
        that works for a mid-handicap golfer — and it is not the order the industry advertises.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>A fitting</strong>, even for clubs you already own. Lie angle and shaft alone can change ball flight substantially.</li>
        <li><strong>Wedges</strong>, if yours are worn. Grooves lose spin, and worn wedges cost you short-game control every round.</li>
        <li><strong>A putter that fits your stroke</strong>, which is a fitting question rather than a price question.</li>
        <li><strong>Golf balls</strong> matched to your swing speed. The cheapest meaningful change available.</li>
        <li><strong>A driver</strong>, last. It is the most expensive club and the one you hit fourteen times a round.</li>
      </ol>
  </div>

  <ChildGrid items={items} heading="Guides in this section" />
</Pillar>

<style>
  .pil-prose { max-width: var(--w-prose); }
  .pil-prose :global(h2) { margin-top: var(--s-7); font-size: var(--fs-xl); }
  .pil-prose :global(li) { margin-bottom: var(--s-2); }
  .pil-prose :global(li::marker) { color: var(--brand); }
  .pil-prose :global(.table-scroll) { max-width: var(--w-content); }
</style>
